{
  "dom_id": "step-q67622",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $2$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273867/assets/b5b7cefccb376e47581c1e858d8e1afaa707cfa6b87518da42a826ce58dd341c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
